WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.637
Staged rollout lets synchronized users
in a federated domain sign in at Entra

2
00:00:05.637 --> 00:00:11.138
ID with CBA instead of being sent to AD
FS, while everyone else stays federated.

3
00:00:11.138 --> 00:00:14.867
Microsoft is explicit that it's
a temporary testing mechanism,

4
00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:19.674
not a permanent mixed state, and its limits
are tighter than the old guide implied.

5
00:00:19.674 --> 00:00:24.157
It applies only to users synced by
Entra Connect, and only to browser and

6
00:00:24.157 --> 00:00:29.475
modern authentication; legacy authentication
still goes to the federation server.

7
00:00:29.475 --> 00:00:33.437
Up to ten groups per feature,
and no nested or dynamic groups.

8
00:00:33.437 --> 00:00:37.124
A group added for the first time
is limited to two hundred users,

9
00:00:37.124 --> 00:00:40.526
and later membership changes
take up to twenty-four hours.

10
00:00:40.526 --> 00:00:45.475
Password sign-in for CBA pilot users
works only with password hash sync.

11
00:00:45.475 --> 00:00:49.266
And issue a Temporary Access
Pass right after adding a user,

12
00:00:49.266 --> 00:00:53.212
so the first sign-in doesn't fall
back to the federation server.

13
00:00:53.212 --> 00:00:56.895
The program pilots with cloud
groups of ten to twenty users,

14
00:00:56.895 --> 00:01:01.631
confirms in the sign-in log which binding
matched and what level was granted,

15
00:01:01.631 --> 00:01:04.412
and grows in steps until
the domain converts.

16
00:01:04.412 --> 00:01:09.456
Moving off AD FS certificate authentication
doesn't change the credential or

17
00:01:09.456 --> 00:01:14.500
the PKI; only the place where authentication
and revocation are checked moves.

18
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.873
Get trust parity, with
internet-reachable CRLs.

19
00:01:17.873 --> 00:01:22.284
Get binding parity, syncing
account mappings to certificate

20
00:01:22.284 --> 00:01:26.262
user IDs where AD FS used them,
and protect that sync path.

21
00:01:26.262 --> 00:01:30.094
Keep staged rollout short,
because staged users stay exposed

22
00:01:30.094 --> 00:01:33.925
to a federation server compromise
until the domain converts.

23
00:01:33.925 --> 00:01:37.450
Record the federation
configuration and back up the farm.

24
00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:40.912
And the old guide called
conversion one-way; it isn't.

25
00:01:40.912 --> 00:01:43.544
Microsoft's rollback is to re-federate,

26
00:01:43.544 --> 00:01:47.230
which works only while AD FS
can still be brought back.
